    concept: "A shared kitchen where every household stores their own spices in the SAME communal spice rack works fine until two households need different amounts of the same spice at the same time, or one family's spice blend conflicts with another's recipe entirely. Installing Python packages globally onto your one system-wide Python installation has exactly this problem: Project A might need version 1.0 of a library while Project B needs version 2.0, and a single shared installation can't satisfy both at once. A virtual environment gives each project its OWN private, isolated set of installed packages - like each household getting their own separate spice cabinet.\n\nConcretely: install Python (3.11 or later is a reasonable modern default), verify it with `python --version` (or `python3 --version` on some systems, where `python` alone may point to an old Python 2 installation), then create a virtual environment per project with `python -m venv myenv` and activate it before installing anything project-specific with `pip` (Python's package installer). Once activated, `pip install requests` installs into THAT project's isolated environment only, never touching your system-wide Python or any other project's packages.\n\nThe interactive REPL (Read-Eval-Print Loop, started by just typing `python` with no filename) is genuinely useful for quickly testing a small expression or exploring a library's behavior without creating a whole .py file - but any real program you intend to keep and run again belongs in a proper `.py` script file, run with `python script.py`, not typed fresh into the REPL every single time.",
