    concept: "Most languages (Java, C, JavaScript) use curly braces `{ }` to mark where a block of code begins and ends - indentation in those languages is purely a courtesy to human readers; the compiler genuinely doesn't care. Python made a deliberate, different design choice: indentation itself IS the block structure. `if condition:` followed by an indented line means that line is INSIDE the if-block; the moment a line returns to the previous (lower) indentation level, Python considers the block closed - there's no separate closing symbol needed at all.\n\nThis has a real, practical consequence: mixing tabs and spaces for indentation within the same file is a genuine syntax error in Python 3, not just a style inconsistency a linter might warn about - Python can't reliably tell whether a tab and some number of spaces represent the 'same' indentation level, so it refuses to guess. The universally recommended convention is 4 spaces per indentation level, configured directly in your editor so pressing Tab actually inserts spaces, not a literal tab character.\n\nEvery Python script is really just a sequence of statements executed top to bottom - unlike Java, there's no REQUIRED top-level class or specific method Python looks for to 'start' the program; execution simply begins at the very first line of the file and proceeds downward, with function/class DEFINITIONS only creating the reusable structure to be called later, not running immediately just because they're defined.",
