    prerequisites: ["Data Types"],
    concept: "Python's arithmetic operators mostly match what you'd expect from a calculator (+, -, *, /), plus two genuinely Python-flavored additions: `//` (floor division - divides and rounds DOWN to the nearest whole number, `7 // 2` is `3`, not `3.5`) and `**` (power/exponent, `2 ** 10` is `1024`, no separate function call needed). Comparison operators (`==`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=`) and logical operators (`and`, `or`, `not` - spelled out as actual words, not symbols like Java's `&&`/`||`/`!`) round out the basics.\n\nThe single most important Python-specific distinction, genuinely worth getting right early: `==` checks whether two values are EQUAL in content, while `is` checks whether two variables refer to the EXACT SAME object in memory (identity, not equality) - `a == b` can be True while `a is b` is False, if they're two separate but equal-content objects. For small integers and short strings, Python's implementation happens to REUSE the same cached objects (similar to Java's String pooling), which is precisely why `is` can misleadingly APPEAR to work for equality checks in casual small-scale testing, then break unexpectedly on larger values or objects built at runtime - `is` should almost always be reserved specifically for comparing against `None` (`x is None`), never as a general substitute for `==`.\n\nOperator precedence follows standard math conventions (`**` binds tighter than `*`/`/`, which bind tighter than `+`/`-`) - `2 + 3 * 2 ** 2` is `14` (power first, then multiplication, then addition), not evaluated strictly left to right. When genuinely unsure, or when writing code someone else will read later, parentheses make the intended order explicit rather than relying on everyone remembering the exact precedence table.",
