    difficulty: "Beginner", estimatedMinutes: 20,
    whatYoullLearn: ["if/elif/else and Python's exact keyword spelling (elif, not 'else if')", "Truthy and falsy values - what Python considers True/False beyond the boolean type itself", "The conditional (ternary) expression: value_if_true if condition else value_if_false"],
    prerequisites: ["Syntax & Indentation"],
    concept: "Python's conditional structure works the same way as any if/else-if/else chain covered elsewhere in this platform (first matching condition wins, top to bottom) - the one genuinely Python-specific detail is the keyword spelling: it's `elif`, not `else if` (no space, contracted) - writing `else if` is a real syntax error in Python, unlike languages where that two-word form is standard.\n\nA distinctly Python idea worth understanding well: 'truthiness'. Python doesn't require an explicit boolean expression in an `if` - ANY value can be tested for truthiness, and a specific, memorable set of values are considered 'falsy' (treated as False in a boolean context): `0`, `0.0`, `\"\"` (empty string), `[]` (empty list), `{}` (empty dict), `None`, and `False` itself. EVERYTHING else - including any non-empty string, any non-zero number, any non-empty collection - is 'truthy'. This is exactly why `if my_list:` is idiomatic, common Python for 'if the list is non-empty', rather than the more verbose `if len(my_list) > 0:` - both work identically, but the truthy version is the style experienced Python developers actually write and expect to read.\n\nThe conditional expression (`x if condition else y`) is Python's version of a ternary operator, but reads notably closer to plain English than Java's `condition ? x : y` syntax - `status = \"Adult\" if age >= 18 else \"Minor\"` reads almost like the actual sentence describing the logic.",
    keyPoints: ["Python's keyword is 'elif' (contracted), not 'else if' - writing 'else if' is a genuine syntax error", "Falsy values: 0, 0.0, \"\" (empty string), [] (empty list), {} (empty dict), None, and False itself - everything else is truthy", "The conditional expression 'x if condition else y' is Python's ternary - idiomatically preferred over verbose if/else for a single simple value decision"],
    commonMistakes: ["Writing 'else if' instead of 'elif' - a habit carried over from other languages that is a genuine syntax error in Python", "Writing `if len(my_list) > 0:` instead of the more idiomatic `if my_list:` - not wrong, just less Pythonic style that experienced Python developers will flag in review", "Assuming only True/False literally count as boolean-context values, missing that 0, empty collections, and None are ALSO falsy"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to list Python's falsy values from memory - a genuinely common, precise Python-specific question", "Know the conditional expression syntax (x if condition else y) cold - it's used constantly in idiomatic Python code, including inside list comprehensions covered later in this path"],
    realWorldApplications: ["Idiomatic Python code relies on truthiness constantly - checking `if response:` after an API call (falsy if the response is empty/None) is standard, everyday Python style"],
    codeExample: { language: "python", code: "age = 20\nif age < 13:\n    category = \"Child\"\nelif age < 18:\n    category = \"Teen\"\nelse:\n    category = \"Adult\"\nprint(category)\n\n# Truthiness and the conditional expression together\nnames = []\nmessage = \"No names yet\" if not names else f\"{len(names)} names\"\nprint(message)  # No names yet - an empty list is falsy" },
