    whatYoullLearn: ["The for loop's real behavior in Python - iterating over a sequence, not counting like Java/C's for loop", "The while loop for condition-driven repetition", "break, continue, and the lesser-known for/else and while/else"],
    prerequisites: ["Conditional Statements"],
    concept: "This is worth being explicit about because it genuinely surprises people coming from Java or C: Python's `for` loop doesn't count through numbers by default the way `for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)` does - it ITERATES directly over the elements of any sequence (a list, a string, a range). `for fruit in [\"apple\", \"banana\"]:` gives you each actual fruit string directly, one at a time; to get a Java-style counting loop, you explicitly loop over `range(10)` (which produces the numbers 0 through 9) instead: `for i in range(10):`.\n\n`while` works the same conceptually as in any language - repeats its body as long as a condition stays true, best when you don't know the exact number of iterations in advance. `break` exits the loop entirely; `continue` skips the rest of the current iteration and moves to the next one - identical behavior to every other language covered on this platform.\n\nA genuinely Python-specific, lesser-known feature: BOTH `for` and `while` loops support an optional `else` clause, which runs ONLY if the loop completed normally, WITHOUT hitting a `break`. This is useful for a 'search and confirm not found' pattern: loop through looking for something, `break` immediately if found, and let the `else` clause run only in the genuine 'searched everything, found nothing' case - avoiding a separate manually-tracked 'found' flag variable.",
    keyPoints: ["Python's for loop iterates directly over a sequence's elements (a list, string, or range) - it doesn't count by default like Java/C's for loop", "range(n) produces the numbers 0 through n-1, giving you a Java-style counting loop when you explicitly want one", "for/while loops support an optional else clause that runs only if the loop completes WITHOUT hitting a break - useful for search-and-confirm-not-found logic"],
    commonMistakes: ["Writing an unnecessary manual index counter to loop through a list, when `for item in my_list:` already gives you each item directly, with no index bookkeeping needed at all", "Not realizing for/while's optional else clause exists, and instead manually tracking a separate boolean 'found' flag for search-and-confirm patterns"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to explain how Python's for loop genuinely differs from Java/C's counting for loop - a common, real conceptual-understanding question for anyone coming from another language", "Know the for/else pattern for search logic - a distinctly Python feature that signals real fluency, not just surface syntax knowledge, when you use it correctly"],
    realWorldApplications: ["Iterating directly over a list of records, a dictionary's items, or a file's lines (covered in File Handling) - Python's for loop is used constantly in exactly this direct-iteration style throughout real, idiomatic code"],
    codeExample: { language: "python", code: "for fruit in [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\"]:\n    print(fruit)  # each fruit directly - no index needed\n\nfor i in range(5):\n    print(i)  # 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 - Java-style counting, made explicit\n\n# for/else - the else runs only if 'break' was never hit\ntarget = 7\nnumbers = [1, 3, 5, 9]\nfor n in numbers:\n    if n == target:\n        print(\"Found it!\")\n        break\nelse:\n    print(\"Not found in the list\")  # runs since 7 isn't in numbers" },
