    prerequisites: ["Lists"],
    concept: "A guest list where the host doesn't care about ARRIVAL ORDER, only WHO'S actually coming, and would never write the same guest's name twice even if they were invited through two different channels - that's a Python `set`: an UNORDERED collection that automatically eliminates duplicates, created with curly braces (`{1, 2, 3}`) or from an existing list (`set([1, 2, 2, 3])` becomes `{1, 2, 3}` - the duplicate 2 simply collapses away).\n\nSets support genuine mathematical set operations directly as operators: `|` (union - everything in EITHER set), `&` (intersection - only what's in BOTH sets), `-` (difference - in the first set but NOT the second). These read almost exactly like set notation from math class, and solve a real, common category of problem elegantly: 'which students are enrolled in BOTH Math and Physics' is just `math_students & physics_students`.\n\nChoosing a set over a list is really answering two questions at once: 'do I need to guarantee no duplicates' and 'do I need fast membership testing' (`x in my_set` is close to O(1) average, versus O(n) for `x in my_list`, which has to check every single element one by one) - if EITHER answer is yes and you don't need to preserve a specific ORDER (sets have none) or allow duplicates (sets forbid them), a set is very likely the better-fitting tool for the job.",
    keyPoints: ["A set is an unordered collection that automatically eliminates duplicates - created with {} or set(existing_list)", "| is union, & is intersection, - is difference - genuine mathematical set operations available directly as operators", "Sets give near-O(1) average membership testing (x in my_set) versus a list's O(n) - the right choice when you need fast lookups and don't care about order or duplicates"],
    commonMistakes: ["Using an empty {} expecting a set - {} actually creates an empty DICTIONARY in Python, not a set; an empty set specifically needs `set()`", "Reaching for a list when you actually need duplicate-free membership testing at scale - a set would be both more correct (guarantees uniqueness) and faster (near-O(1) lookup) for that exact use case"],
    interviewTips: ["Be ready to explain why `x in my_set` is faster than `x in my_list` for large collections - a genuine, practical performance-reasoning question, not just trivia", "Know the {} versus set() distinction for creating an empty set - a frequently-tested, easy-to-miss Python-specific gotcha"],
    realWorldApplications: ["Deduplicating a list of email addresses, or finding common elements between two groups (like 'students enrolled in both course A and course B') are everyday real uses of sets and their operators"],
    codeExample: { language: "python", code: "math_students = {\"Asha\", \"Ravi\", \"Meera\"}\nphysics_students = {\"Ravi\", \"Karan\"}\n\nprint(math_students | physics_students)  # union - everyone in either course\nprint(math_students & physics_students)  # intersection - {'Ravi'} - in both\nprint(math_students - physics_students)  # difference - in math only, not physics\n\nempty_set = set()   # NOT {} - that creates an empty dict instead" },
