    concept: "Same distinction as this platform's Java path: product-company technical interviews go deeper and combine topics more than placement-drive questions, expecting genuine reasoning, not just recall.\n\nA strong Python interview answer follows the identical three-part shape used throughout this whole course: precise definition, a concrete example, and the key trade-off or distinction from something commonly confused with it. Asked 'what's the difference between a list and a generator', a strong answer states both precisely, gives a concrete large-file or large-range example, AND states the actual trade-off (memory efficiency versus the ability to re-iterate or index directly) - not just the bare definitions.\n\nThe handful of topics worth having rock-solid, because they appear across nearly every Python interview regardless of company or role: == vs is, mutable default arguments (and why they're a trap), list/tuple/dict/set - which to use when and why, generators vs lists (memory trade-off), decorators (what they are and a concrete use case), and Python's GIL (Global Interpreter Lock - a genuinely Python-specific, frequently-asked concept worth knowing at a high level even before any dedicated lesson: it means only one thread executes Python bytecode at a time in the standard CPython implementation, which is why CPU-bound multithreading in Python doesn't parallelize the way it does in Java, though I/O-bound work still benefits from threading, and true CPU parallelism uses separate PROCESSES instead via the multiprocessing module).",
    keyPoints: ["Product-company interviews go deeper and combine topics more than placement-drive questions - expect 'why' follow-ups, not just recall", "Strong answer structure: precise definition, concrete example, AND the key trade-off/distinction - identical structure to every other subject on this platform", "Highest-frequency topics worth having rock-solid: == vs is, mutable default arguments, list/tuple/dict/set choice, generators vs lists, decorators, and the GIL's high-level implication for threading"],
    commonMistakes: ["Answering only with a memorized definition when the interviewer clearly wants the underlying REASONING or trade-off - depth of understanding, not recall alone, is being evaluated at this level", "Not knowing what the GIL is at even a high level - a distinctly Python-specific concept that a candidate with only surface familiarity is likely to be caught unprepared for"],
    interviewTips: ["Practice explaining every item in this lesson's 'rock-solid' list out loud, in under 60 seconds each, with a concrete example - not just silently reading and nodding along", "When you genuinely don't know something, say precisely what you DO know and reason toward the rest out loud - this consistently scores better than guessing or freezing"],
